<h2>1. Claude — Best for Research, Writing and Code in One Place</h2>
<p><strong>Rating: 4.7/5</strong></p>
<p>For an early team, the practical advantage of Claude is that one subscription covers the three things a small company does constantly: reading long documents, drafting and rewriting, and writing actual code. The free tier includes chat, file creation, code execution, web search, memory and connectors; Pro adds more usage, Claude Code, Projects and the Opus models.</p>
<p><strong>Pricing:</strong> Free $0 · Pro $20/mo ($17/mo billed yearly, $200 up front) · Max $100/mo · Ultra $200/mo. Team plans start at $25/seat/mo monthly ($20/seat/mo annual) with SSO and central billing.</p>
<p><strong>Free tier:</strong> Yes — limited every five hours and weekly, but functional for a solo founder.</p>
<p><strong>Who it's for:</strong> Founders who want one assistant for docs and code. <strong>Not for:</strong> teams that need audited, org-wide controls — that is the Team or Enterprise tier.</p>
<p class="flex items-center gap-2 flex-wrap">
  <a href="/tool/claude" class="btn-primary">See Claude in the directory →</a>
</p>

<h2>2. Bolt — Best for Shipping an MVP This Week</h2>
<p><strong>Rating: 4.5/5</strong></p>
<p>Bolt turns a prompt into a running web app with hosting attached, which is the fastest path from "we should validate this" to a link you can send to a customer. The free plan is unusually usable: 1M tokens a month with a 300K daily cap, unlimited databases and hosting, with Bolt branding on published sites. Pro removes the branding, adds a custom domain and SEO controls, and starts at 10M tokens a month with rollover.</p>
<p><strong>Pricing:</strong> Free $0 · Pro $25/mo · Teams $30/member/mo · Enterprise custom. Tokens are per-member on Teams, not pooled.</p>
<p><strong>Free tier:</strong> Yes — 1M tokens/month, 300K/day, no card required.</p>
<p><strong>Who it's for:</strong> Non-technical founders and small teams validating a product. <strong>Not for:</strong> production systems that need a real data model and permissions — plan the handover to a developer before launch.</p>
<p class="flex items-center gap-2 flex-wrap">
  <a href="/tool/bolt" class="btn-primary">See Bolt in the directory →</a>
</p>

<h2>3. Notion — Best for Docs, Specs and a Knowledge Base That Answers Back</h2>
<p><strong>Rating: 4.4/5</strong></p>
<p>Notion's role at seed stage is to stop knowledge living in three people's heads. The AI story matters mostly from Business upward, where the Notion Agent handles multi-step tasks using Notion content plus connected apps, AI Meeting Notes automates follow-ups, and Enterprise Search reaches into Slack and GitHub.</p>
<p><strong>Pricing:</strong> Free $0 · Plus $10/member/mo · Business $20/member/mo · Enterprise custom. Notion AI is a trial on Free and Plus, and included on Business and above.</p>
<p><strong>Free tier:</strong> Yes — unlimited pages for solo use, with a block limit once the workspace has multiple members.</p>
<p><strong>Who it's for:</strong> Teams writing specs, onboarding docs and a public-facing help centre. <strong>Not for:</strong> anyone who mainly needs a database with an API — the AI is the reason to choose Notion, not the storage.</p>
<p class="flex items-center gap-2 flex-wrap">
  <a href="/tool/notion-ai" class="btn-primary">See Notion AI in the directory →</a>
</p>

<h2>4. Synthesia — Best for a Product Demo That Sells While You Sleep</h2>
<p><strong>Rating: 4.5/5</strong></p>
<p>The demo video is the most underrated asset at seed stage: it answers the same six questions in every sales call, and it works on a landing page at 2am. Synthesia produces presenter-led explainers from a script, with 125+ avatars on Starter, 140+ languages and a re-record flow that means changing the script rather than reshooting. Compliance certifications (SOC 2 Type II, ISO 27001) matter to any startup selling into enterprises.</p>
<p><strong>Pricing:</strong> Free Basic (10 min/mo, watermarked, 9 avatars) · Starter {SYNTHESIA_PRICING.starter.price} ({SYNTHESIA_PRICING.starter.annual} billed yearly, {SYNTHESIA_PRICING.starter.annualTotal} — {SYNTHESIA_PRICING.starter.credits} credits, about 12 minutes of footage) · Pro {SYNTHESIA_PRICING.pro.price} ({SYNTHESIA_PRICING.pro.annual} billed yearly, {SYNTHESIA_PRICING.pro.credits} credits, 5 personal avatars) · Enterprise custom.</p>
<p><strong>Free tier:</strong> Yes — about 10 minutes a month, watermarked.</p>
<p><strong>Who it's for:</strong> Founders who need a demo, an onboarding video and a localised version for each market. <strong>Not for:</strong> brand films with cinematic ambitions.</p>
<p class="flex items-center gap-2 flex-wrap">
  <AffiliateBtn slug="synthesia" text="Start Free with Synthesia" loc="startups-listicle" />
  <a href="/synthesia-review" class="text-sm font-medium text-[#3B82F6] hover:underline">Read the full Synthesia review →</a>
</p>

<h2>5. ElevenLabs — Best for In-Product Voice and Narration</h2>
<p><strong>Rating: 4.7/5</strong></p>
<p>There are two distinct uses at a startup. The obvious one is narration for demo and marketing video. The less obvious one is product voice — if whatever you are building speaks, ElevenLabs is usually cheaper and faster than hiring voice talent for every iteration, and its API makes voice a feature rather than an asset. Language coverage depends on the model — {ELEVENLABS_LANGS.inline} — with cloning from short samples.</p>
<p><strong>Pricing:</strong> Free ({ELEVENLABS_PRICING.free.credits} credits/mo) · Starter {ELEVENLABS_PRICING.starter.price} ({ELEVENLABS_PRICING.starter.credits} credits) · Creator {ELEVENLABS_PRICING.creator.price} ({ELEVENLABS_PRICING.creator.credits} credits, professional voice cloning) · Pro {ELEVENLABS_PRICING.pro.price} · Scale {ELEVENLABS_PRICING.scale.price} · Business {ELEVENLABS_PRICING.business.price}.</p>
<p><strong>Free tier:</strong> Yes — 10,000 characters a month, roughly ten minutes of finished speech.</p>
<p><strong>Who it's for:</strong> Products that speak, and teams producing narrated video at volume. <strong>Not for:</strong> anything requiring talent-rights guarantees on a cloned voice — sort licensing before shipping.</p>
<p class="flex items-center gap-2 flex-wrap">
  <AffiliateBtn slug="elevenlabs" text="Start Free on ElevenLabs" loc="startups-listicle" />
  <a href="/elevenlabs-review" class="text-sm font-medium text-[#3B82F6] hover:underline">Read the full ElevenLabs review →</a>
</p>

<h2>6. Canva — Best for the Deck, the Site Mock and the Social Assets</h2>
<p><strong>Rating: 4.6/5</strong></p>
<p>Startups produce a disproportionate amount of visual work with no designer: a deck, a one-pager, ad variants, social posts, event banners. Canva's free tier covers 1.6M+ templates, a single brand kit and a limited AI allowance; paid tiers add brand kits at scale, approvals, social scheduling and a much larger AI allowance. The reason to pay is volume and consistency, not capability.</p>
<p><strong>Pricing:</strong> Free $0 · Pro and Business available monthly or billed annually. Pro is single-user; Business adds team admin, approvals and more AI per seat.</p>
<p><strong>Free tier:</strong> Yes — genuinely usable for a first deck and social presence.</p>
<p><strong>Who it's for:</strong> Founders doing their own design until a designer exists. <strong>Not for:</strong> brand systems that need print-grade control.</p>
<p class="flex items-center gap-2 flex-wrap">
  <a href="/tool/canva" class="btn-primary">See Canva in the directory →</a>
</p>

<h2>7. Surfer — Best for Getting Found in Google and in AI Answers</h2>
<p><strong>Rating: 4.3/5</strong></p>
<p>The channel that changed in 2026 is discovery: buyers increasingly ask an assistant instead of scrolling a results page, so being mentioned in ChatGPT or Perplexity is now a distribution problem, not an SEO vanity metric. Surfer scores drafts against live SERP structure and, from the AI Search Analytics tier, tracks whether your brand is mentioned at all.</p>
<p><strong>Pricing:</strong> Discovery $49/mo (120 documents, 10 tracked pages) · Standard $99/mo (360 documents, 25 prompts weekly) · Pro $182/mo (50 prompts daily, 200 pages) · Peace of Mind $299/mo · Enterprise custom.</p>
<p><strong>Free tier:</strong> None — start at Discovery and upgrade when tracked pages run out.</p>
<p><strong>Who it's for:</strong> Startups with a content-led acquisition plan. <strong>Not for:</strong> pre-product teams — nothing to optimise yet.</p>
<p class="flex items-center gap-2 flex-wrap">
  <a href="/tool/surfer-seo" class="btn-primary">See Surfer in the directory →</a>
</p>

<h2>8. HubSpot — Best Free CRM, With a Real Cost Cliff Above It</h2>
<p><strong>Rating: 4.4/5</strong></p>
<p>The free tier is the reason most startups end up here: CRM, forms, email and live chat for two users, no credit card. Starter adds credits, Agent Hub and ad management for a low per-seat price. The jump to Professional is the one to plan for — automation, social and video tooling arrive there, along with a monthly commitment and a one-time onboarding fee.</p>
<p><strong>Pricing:</strong> Free $0 · Starter $20/mo/seat (frequently discounted) · Professional $890/mo monthly ($800/mo billed annually, plus a one-time $3,000 onboarding fee) · Enterprise $3,600/mo (plus $7,000 onboarding).</p>
<p><strong>Free tier:</strong> Yes — two users, full CRM basics, no card.</p>
<p><strong>Who it's for:</strong> Teams that will eventually need marketing and sales in one database. <strong>Not for:</strong> a founder tracking 30 contacts — a spreadsheet and an assistant is cheaper, and the migration cost is real.</p>
<p class="flex items-center gap-2 flex-wrap">
  <a href="/tool/hubspot-ai" class="btn-primary">See HubSpot in the directory →</a>
</p>

<h2>9. Zapier — Best for Automating the Glue Nobody Wants to Own</h2>
<p><strong>Rating: 4.5/5</strong></p>
<p>Support email into the CRM, new signup into Slack, form submission into a sheet and a notification — these are ten-minute automations that quietly cost hours a week when done by hand. Zapier's free tier allows 100 tasks a month and two-step Zaps, which is enough to prove a workflow before paying; paid plans scale by task volume rather than seats.</p>
<p><strong>Pricing:</strong> Free (100 tasks/mo, two-step Zaps) · paid plans from $19.99/mo.</p>
<p><strong>Free tier:</strong> Yes — 100 tasks a month.</p>
<p><strong>Who it's for:</strong> Teams running three or more tools that need to share data. <strong>Not for:</strong> high-volume event pipelines — at that point the economics favour direct API work.</p>
<p class="flex items-center gap-2 flex-wrap">
  <a href="/tool/zapier" class="btn-primary">See Zapier in the directory →</a>
</p>

