<h2>How to Choose</h2>
<p><strong>Pre-product (week 1-4):</strong> Claude free tier plus Bolt free. You need a running prototype and something to send it with; that costs $0 until you hit Bolt's 1M-token monthly cap.</p>
<p><strong>First paying customers:</strong> add HubSpot's free CRM before you need it, not after. Migrating 200 contacts is easy, migrating a year of messy pipeline is not. Then add Zapier's free tier to get form submissions into the CRM automatically.</p>
<p><strong>Starting to sell:</strong> the demo video is the highest-leverage asset. Synthesia's free plan gives about 10 watermarked minutes; Starter at {SYNTHESIA_PRICING.starter.price} removes the watermark and adds about 12 minutes of footage a month, which covers a demo plus two feature explainers.</p>
<p><strong>Starting to publish:</strong> Surfer at $49/mo only makes sense once there is a product page worth ranking and someone to write. Before that, spend the money on the demo instead.</p>
<p><strong>What to skip:</strong> per-seat tools bought for people who will not log in, annual commitments before a workflow is validated, and any tool whose only benefit is that it appeared in a listicle. Audit seats monthly in the first year, not annually.</p>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>What AI tools does a startup actually need on day one?</h3>
<p>Four, and three can be free: a reasoning assistant (Claude free or $20/month for Pro), a place to write things down (Notion free, or Business at $20/member/month for the Notion Agent), a way to ship a working prototype (Bolt free gives 1M tokens a month, Pro is $25/month) and an automation layer (Zapier free covers 100 tasks a month). Everything else waits until a specific bottleneck appears.</p>

<h3>Is it cheaper to build an MVP with AI tools or hire a developer?</h3>
<p>For a validation build, AI builders are dramatically cheaper — Bolt Pro is $25/month, with Lovable and Cursor selling comparable monthly tiers. The trade-off is ceiling, not price: they get you to a testable product fast and then stop being the right tool once you need a real data model, permissions and payments. Most teams validate with AI and productionise with a developer.</p>

<h3>Do startups need AI video and voice tools?</h3>
<p>Earlier than most founders expect. A demo that does not require a call is now part of the sales motion: Synthesia's Starter is {SYNTHESIA_PRICING.starter.price} ({SYNTHESIA_PRICING.starter.annual} billed yearly) for about 12 minutes of avatar footage, and the free plan allows about 10 watermarked minutes a month. ElevenLabs starts at {ELEVENLABS_PRICING.starter.price} with a {ELEVENLABS_PRICING.free.credits}-credit free tier.</p>

<h3>How much should a seed-stage startup budget for AI subscriptions?</h3>
<p>Roughly $100-200 a month at pre-seed, and it should stay under $500 until there is revenue. A working set is $20-25 for an assistant, $0-20 for docs, $25-49 for building, $0-19 for automation and $0-6 for voice. The line item that quietly inflates is per-seat tools bought for people who never log in.</p>

<h3>Which AI tools have free tiers good enough that you do not need to pay?</h3>
<p>Bolt's free plan is 1M tokens a month with a 300K daily cap — enough for a prototype. Notion free is unlimited pages for solo use, HubSpot free covers two users with no card, Canva free covers 1.6M+ templates and one brand kit, Zapier free is 100 tasks a month, and both Claude and ElevenLabs run usable free tiers. Together they carry a two-person team a long way.</p>

<h2>How We Evaluate</h2>
<p>This page is a buyer's guide, not a lab report: tools are compared on documented features, official pricing and published limits. We do not present lab-style test results we did not produce.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Scope:</strong> The jobs that consume a seed-stage team's time — build, document, demo, sell, publish, automate.</li>
<li><strong>Method:</strong> Feature lists, official pricing pages and vendor-published usage limits, checked on the date shown above.</li>
<li><strong>Criteria:</strong> Whether the free tier can carry a real workload, the cost of the next tier up, seat and token maths, and how quickly the tool becomes dead weight if the company pivots.</li>
<li><strong>Corrections:</strong> Prices move. If a figure here disagrees with the vendor's page, email admin@aizest.net and we will fix it.</li>
</ul>

